# Copy to .env and fill in. Loaded automatically by run.sh.
#
# NOTE: this file is named `env.example` (NO leading dot). Eleven of the repo's
# thirteen sample templates ship as `.env.example`; this one and
# `mcp-connections/env.example` are the two exceptions. There is no technical
# reason for the difference: it was introduced under the belief that `.env.*`
# files could not be written in the authoring environment, which the 2026-08-03
# docs audit disproved (it edited eight dotted templates in place that day).
# Renaming sample files is a structural change, deferred to that audit's T10 —
# see cowork/DOCS-TRUTH-LEDGER.md, escalation E23. Copy it as:
#
#     cp env.example .env    # then edit
#
# Infra (endpoint/host/tenant/creds) is auto-resolved by ../lib/resolve-env.sh —
# only secrets and driver overrides go here. Provisioning itself is declarative
# now (manifest.yaml + `yoizen manifests apply`); see README.md.

# =============================================================================
# Secret VALUES — manifest.yaml's `secrets:` binding names are hyphenated
# (`mcp-repo-support-bot-openai-api-key` / `mcp-repo-support-bot-telegram-
# token`), not valid bash identifiers for a sourced .env, so these plain-name
# vars are the user-facing inputs; the "Provision" command in README.md remaps
# them to the exact binding names via `env "<binding-name>=$VALUE"` at apply
# time (no naming transform on the BINDING itself — decision 7 — this is just
# how a hyphenated binding name is supplied from a sourced .env file).
# =============================================================================
# REQUIRED — a real OpenAI key; both agents need a working LLM connector.
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# REQUIRED for outbound Telegram delivery (from @BotFather). ANY non-empty
# value provisions successfully; only real delivery needs a real token.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456:ABC-your-bot-token

# =============================================================================
# The RUN driver (src/index.ts) — verifies resources, then drives one
# synthetic Telegram exchange end to end (SIMULATE_INBOUND=1 by default).
# =============================================================================
# The account's webhook appSecret (GET /channels/accounts after apply) —
# signs the synthetic inbound update. Required unless SIMULATE_INBOUND=0.
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Set to 0 to only verify the MCP server/agents/workflow exist, without
# driving a synthetic exchange.
# SIMULATE_INBOUND=1
# SIMULATE_TEXT=How does routing work in vercel/next.js?
# TELEGRAM_TEST_CHAT_ID=999999999   # any number for observe-only; a real chat
#                                   # that /start-ed the bot for real delivery.
# TG_POLL_TIMEOUT_S=90

# =============================================================================
# Naming overrides — only needed if you renamed resources in manifest.yaml.
# =============================================================================
# REPO_NAME=vercel/next.js
# DEEPWIKI_SERVER_NAME=deepwiki
# TRIAGE_AGENT_NAME=repo-support-triage
# SUMMARIZER_AGENT_NAME=repo-support-summarizer
# WORKFLOW_NAME=mcp-repo-support-bot
# TG_EXTERNAL_ID=manifest:mcp-repo-support-bot
